      "writtenOn": "2021-10-19T17:59:05Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e I don\u0027t get the reason for intersect(pt) to return false from a theoretic standpoint, unless it\u0027s just there to allow the user to choose?\n\nMy reasoning is that if you have two side-by-side rects with a shared edge, intersects() is false:\n\n  e.g. - ltrb\u003d[0,0,1,1] and ltrb\u003d[1,0,2,1]\n  \nThey have a shared edge, but they do not intersect. If you shrink the rect on the right infinitesimally small, intersects() is still false. So if you continue shrinking it all the way to empty, I think intersects should continue to be false. Conversely, contains() returns true if there is a shared edge.\n\nYou can see this distinction in their implementations -- intersects() already uses \u003c and contains uses \u003c\u003d. If you call intersects and/or contains with an empty point-rect today, they will have the same behavior I described previously. Being able to choose different edge behaviors based on which one you call is mostly just a perk on top of how these methods are already defined. (This is why there is a disclaimer on this class saying \"We never check for empty! Look how the methods are defined and think about what that means if you want to use an empty or negative rect!\")\n\nI think all this ambiguity is an argument in favor of not providing point containment methods, and instead requiring the caller to create empty rects explicitly and think about what they\u0027re doing. Can we add a Rect::Point() factory?\n\n(Plus I think it would be confusing if methods with the same name behaved differently depending on whether you used the point overload or the rect overload with an empty rect.)",
      "parentUuid": "fb45ee93_9254d4ed",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 95,
        "startChar": 12,
        "endLine": 95,
        "endChar": 20
      },
      "revId": "f7552afdd47dd81e65636bdb9dbb10af50c7e422",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
